Keywords: Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) Humidified benzene vapor Breakthrough analysis MOF structural stability Kinetic/isotherm analyses
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have opened a new path for the construction of air/gas purification systems through a number of technological routes (e.g., adsorption and/or catalysis). To develop MOFs with upgraded adsorption performance, the breakthrough (BT) behavior of two MOF sorbents (e.g., MOF-199 and UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\cdot$ ) was investigated using gaseous benzene under the interactive conditions between the two key variables: (i) the inlet benzene vapor concentration (10 to $200\mathrm{ppm}$ and (ii) relative humidity $\mathrm{RH}=0$ to $100\%$ ). According to the comparison of adsorption performance derived using gaseous benzene, MOF-199 outperformed UiO- $66\ –\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH_{2}}}$ by 2 to 16-fold when assessed at $10\%$ BT and zero RH. This observation thus reflects the high reactivity of Cu-metal sites for interaction with benzene ring compared with Zr sites. The dynamic adsorption data for the two MOFs at various RH conditions also indicated the competitive inhibition effect of water vapor on benzene adsorption mechanism, especially at low benzene concentrations $(<100\ \mathrm{ppm})$ and high RH level of $>50\%$ . Adsorption kinetics of benzene onto the two MOFs showed good fitting with both pseudo-first and -second-order kinetics at low RH $(0-20\%)$ conditions, whereas the results at high RH $(>50\%)$ ) failed to fit with both kinetics (e.g., $\mathbf{R}^{2}\leq$ 0.87) because of the high roll-up effect. The water vapor acted as a competitive component, reducing the per­ formance of the MOF adsorbents in purifying humidified gaseous streams. That effect became more prominent as the benzene concentration decreased $(\leq50\ \mathrm{ppm})$ , reflecting the dynamic interaction between multiple adsor­ bates (water and benzene) and the adsorbent. In addition, the exothermic adsorption property was evident for both MOFs under the dry conditions, while the patterns were reversed under humid conditions (e.g., $100\%$ RH). Overall, the adsorption properties of VOCs onto MOFs and their uptake mechanisms are affected sensitively by an interplay between the key variables (e.g., concentration levels of competing adsorbates) along with the structural stabilities of sorbents against water vapor.
## 1. Introduction
Benzene is a well-known toxic and carcinogenic pollutant emitted from various industrial sources into atmospheric environments. It is listed as a Group 1 carcinogen to humans by the International Agency for Research on Cancer [1]. A permissible exposure limit of 1 ppm gaseous benzene in the workplace during an 8-hour workday has been set by the U.S. Occupational Safety and Health Administration [2].
Over the past decades, the removal of benzene from the atmosphere, water, and soils has been undertaken with the aid of many techniques (e. g., adsorption [3], photocatalysis [4], and biofiltration [5]). Adsorption is one of the most promising treatment techniques due to its easy operation, flexibility, and low energy requirements [6,7]. As a result, many adsorbents have been developed and used to capture gaseous benzene, such as metal-organic frameworks (MOFs: e.g., MIL-101, HKUST-1 (or MOF-199), and UiO-66) [3,8], activated carbon (AC) [9], biochar [7], and other composites (e.g., diatomite/MFI-type zeolite) [10]. Among them, MOFs have received considerable attention due to their tunable porosity, fast kinetics, high adsorption capacity, adsorp­ tion reversibility, and the large volume-to-surface ratio [11,12]. Almost all of these previous studies were conducted to evaluate the adsorption capacity of MOFs in dry gaseous streams.
B. Liu et al.
In many studies, the adsorbent performance was often evaluated in a biased way, as the experiments were carried out under non-realistic conditions (e.g., significantly high initial loading of the target species) to overestimate the adsorption capacity [3,7,13]. For instance, Fe-doped MOF-199 was used to dynamically adsorb benzene at concentration levels of ${\sim}10\ \mathrm{kPa}$ or ${{10}^{5}}$ ppm under dry conditions [14]. Under those conditions, the modified Fe-MOF-199 recorded the exceptionally large adsorption capacity for benzene of $889.2~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , much higher than pristine MOF-199 $(585\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})$ , which was ascribed to increased mes­ opore filling (diffusion coefficient of $2.85{-}4.07\times10^{-11}\mathrm{cm}^{2}s^{-1})$ . At a relative humidity (RH) of 0, both MOF-199 and UiO-66 were tested and found to be effective sorbents for the removal of $0.1{-}50~\mathrm{ppm}$ gaseous benzene, with high $10\%$ breakthrough volume $10\%$ BTV) values of ${>}280\mathrm{L}$ atm ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and $>60\mathrm{~L~}$ atm ${\bf g}^{-1}$ , respectively [3].
Despite the great potential of MOFs as adsorbents, their effective use in real air-purifying systems is frequently challenging because of their kinetic instability in the presence of moisture [15,16]. In this respect, some studies have begun to address the mechanisms by which MOF structures (e.g., $Z\mathbf{r}$ -based MOFs and Cu-based MOFs) degrade or collapse upon exposure to water vapor in gas streams or humid environments [17–20]. However, a detailed compilation of experimental data on the effects of RH on the adsorption performance of MOFs toward VOCs re­ mains lacking. That shortage of reliable data significantly hinders the understanding of moisture effects on MOFs’ adsorption performance in real-world conditions (e.g., 1 atm, $298\ \mathrm{K},$ and low initial inlet VOC concentrations). Only a few case studies on the subject have been pub­ lished. For example, MIL-101(Cr) underwent a remarkable decrease in its maximum adsorption capacity for dichloroethane, ethyl acetate, and benzene (by $87.6\%$ , $89.6\%$ , and $96.5\%$ , respectively), as RH increased from 0 to $80\%$ [21]. Another study screened ${>}300{,}000$ MOFs to deter­ mine the best adsorbent for separating $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ from $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ in wet flue gases [22]. Among them, Al-PMOF and Al-PyrMOF were selected as the best candidates because their performance was almost completely retained in the presence of water vapor. The adsorption selectivity of MOF-199 for benzene was assessed in the presence of water vapor, and it was found to have a selectivity ratio of 8.32 for benzene/water at $318\mathrm{K}$ and 1.0 mbar [23]. However, almost all those case studies used unrealistically high partial pressure conditions when testing the effects of water molecules. Therefore, it is crucial to acquire experimental data that objectively describe the effects of RH on the adsorption mechanisms of MOFs without exaggerating their performance. In this context, the testing conditions should simulate the concentration levels of VOCs (e.g., ben­ zene) found in real-world gaseous waste streams or ambient conditions (1 atm and 298 K). Building such a database will be important to the design and development of thermodynamically stable MOF-based ad­ sorbents for practical applications.
Herein, an effort has been made to better understand the potential applicability of MOFs as an advanced platform for air purification, especially toward benzene vapor in real-world conditions across varying humidity levels. The dynamic adsorption properties of two MOFs, MOF199 and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , were investigated under various combinations of two key variables: (i) RH levels (0, 20, 50, 80, and $100\%$ ) and (ii) inlet benzene concentrations (10, 50, 100, and $200\mathrm{ppm}\mathrm{,}$ ). The effect of water vapor on the adsorption capability/mechanism of MOFs for benzene was also evaluated based on kinetic, isotherm, and thermodynamic analyses. The overall results of this work are expected to offer valuable insights into how the target VOC pressure and interference from ambient mois­ ture interact to affect the adsorption performance of MOFs, which could expand their practical applicability in the environmental field.
## 2. Materials and experimental procedures
## 2.1. Materials and methods
All the reagents used in this study were commercially available products that were purchased and used without further purification.
## 2.2. Adsorption experimental design
In this research, $20\ \mathrm{L}$ polyester aluminum (PEA: Top Trading Eng., Seoul, Korea) bags filled with ${\bf N}_{2}$ gas $(99.999\%)$ were used to prepare gaseous benzene working standards (G-WSs) at various concentrations and humidity (RH) levels. The preparation of G-WSs at five different RH levels (0, 20, 50, 80, and $100\%$ ) was carried out based on a previously reported procedure [26]. In brief, syringes with two different volumes (10 and $500~{\upmu\mathrm{L}};$ Trajan Scientific and Medical, Ringwood, Australia) were used to inject the required amounts of liquid benzene $(99.5\%$ , Wako Pure Chemical Industries, Ltd., Republic of Korea) that produced the desired concentration levels in the PEA bags. Likewise, varying amounts of pure water (91, 228, 365, and $456\upmu\mathrm{L})$ were injected into the same bags to meet the required RH levels. Subsequently, all the PEA bags (20L) were left at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to allow the injected liquids to fully vaporize into the G-WSs.
## 2.3. Dynamic adsorption experiment
A schematic of the experimental procedures for the dynamic adsorption experiment is provided in Fig. S1. To prepare the adsorption tube system, MOF sorbents $(5~\mathrm{mg})$ were packed into cylindrical sorbent tubes (9 cm length $\times4$ mm inner diameter with thickness of $1\mathrm{mm}^{\cdot}$ ) with the help of quartz wool $(3~\mathrm{mg})$ from each side to hold the sorbent in place during the experimental runs. The quartz wool was used to stably hold the position of MOFs in the sorbent column so that the gas stream can freely pass through the packed bed column for the progress of the adsorption process. In addition, the amount of packed MOF adsorbents (i.e., $5\mathrm{mg}^{\cdot}$ ) was generally suitable to make a thin packed bed (thickness of $1\ \mathrm{mm}\dot{}$ sorbent tube without any void spacing issues. Note that the mass of MOF sorbents in this column was maintained a the lowest level to avoid thepressure drop during the adsorption process [27]. The packed-sorbent tubes were then placed into a thermal conditioning system (CT2000 Tube Cleaner) at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\ensuremath{\mathrm{h}}$ under at a constant flow $(200\ \mathrm{mL}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1})$ of pure $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ $(99.999\%)$ to thermally activate the tubes before the adsorption study. The concentration of benzene in the outlet stream (i.e., breakthrough) was determined as a function of the volume loaded in the G-WS standard. To conduct the quantitative analysis of benzene exiting the adsorption system, the pre-activated packed sam­ pling sorbent tubes were placed between the G-WS bag and a large volume injection (LVI: $330\mathrm{\mL/min^{-1}}.$ ) unit installed on a gas chroma­ tography (GC) system interfaced with a flame ionization detector (FID). During the adsorption process, the LVI/GC-FID system pulled the ben­ zene stream from the G-WS bag through the sorbent tubes at room temperature (i.e., $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Then, the pulled gaseous benzene in LVI sys­ tem was quantitated using the GC/FID (Shimadzu Model GC-2010) technique. Table S1 shows the operating conditions for the LVI/GCFID system.
The experiments at different temperatures were also carried out so as to understand the combinatory effects of temperature and moisture on the adsorption performance of both MOFs against benzene at a fixed concentration $(50\ \mathrm{ppm})$ with varying temperatures (i.e., 25, 40, and $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and both under dry (i.e., $0\%$ RH) and humid (i.e., $100\%$ RH) conditions. In addition, to assess the suitability of MOFs as adsorbents in a practical sense, their reusability was also tested for three adsorptiondesorption cycles against $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ benzene at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under both dry $(0\%$ RH) and humid conditions (i.e., 50 and $100\%$ RH). During this reusability test, the spent MOF sorbents were regenerated under ${\bf N}_{2}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (CT2000 Tube Cleaner) for $3\mathrm{h}$ after each cycle. Note that water adsorption capacities were estimated by monitoring the mass changes between before and after $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ vapor adsorption with the aid of an
