## 2.4. Quality control of adsorption/analytical results
The analytical reliability of the quantification approach used for the target gas (benzene) was assessed by determining basic quality assur­ ance and quality control parameters using a five-point, fixed, standardvolume method, as reported elsewhere [28]. The method detection limit (MDL) for the GC-FID system with a $1\mathrm{mL}$ atm sample injection was 1.6 ng benzene, with a corresponding relative standard error value of $0.51\%$ . The MDL was determined by multiplying the standard deviation value of 7 replicate analyses with the student’s t-value for a $99\%$ con­ fidence interval $(t=3.14)$ ) [29].
The BT $\mathrm{{(C_{\mathrm{{out}}}/C_{\mathrm{{in}}})}}$ was determined by taking the average value of the exiting concentrations (between runs $\scriptstyle{\mathtt{n-1}}$ and $\mathbf{n}$ ) and normalizing it to the inlet concentration (Eq. (1)).
$$
\frac{C_{o u t}}{C_{i n}}=\frac{0.5\left[\left(o u t l e t\ c o n c e n t r a t i o n\right)_{n}+\left(o u t l e t\ c o n c e n t r a t i o n\right)_{n-1}\right]}{I n l e t\ c o n c e n t r a t i o n}
$$
The adsorption performance of the MOFs against benzene vapor was evaluated based on the partition coefficient $\left(\mathbf{PC};\mathbf{mol}\mathbf{kg}^{-1}\mathbf{Pa}^{-1}\right.$ , Eq. (2)), adsorption capacity $\left(\mathsf{Q};\mathsf{m g}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}\right.$ , Eq. (3)), and BT volume (BTV: L atm ${\bf g}^{-1}$ , Eq. (4)) metrics, as follows.
$$
P C\left(a t n\%B T\right)=\frac{Q(a t n\%B T)}{P_{o u t}\left(a t n\%B T\right)}
$$
$$
Q=\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{m}\Delta m_{n}}{M}
$$
$$
B T V(a t n\%)=\frac{\sum\Delta V_{n}}{M}
$$
where $\mathbf{P_{out}}$ is the outlet benzene partial pressure $\mathbf{(Pa)}$ , Q is the benzene mass sorbed at the $\mathtt{n_{t h}}$ run, and $\mathbf{M}$ is the sorbent mass (g).
The equilibrium adsorption behaviors of benzene on MOF-199 and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ across various RH levels $\mathrm{(RH_{\Omega}{=}}0{-}100\%$ ) were analyzed using three isotherm models (Langmuir (L), Freundlich (F), and Dubi­ nin–Radushkevich (DR)) to describe the role of humidity on the adsorption mechanisms. Notably, the isotherm results can be modeled using the BT curves under the assumption that the transient equilibrium states were attained in the adsorption process [30]. More specifically, the isotherm data for this method were derived based on the following assumptions: (i) low-pressure drop is hypothesized throughout the whole adsorption process; (ii) the adsorption is isothermal for the low benzene concentrations; (iii) the ideal gas phase behavior is approxi­ mated; and (iv) the ideal plug flow is assumed for the flow pattern [31–33]. However, it should be noted that the estimated isotherms of an ideal gas under the standard adsorption process (based on the above hypotheses) can provide a biased simulation for the real-world condi­ tions. This is because the real polluted air/gaseous streams contain both ideal (air) and non-ideal (water vapor) gas [34]. Accordingly, the isotherm conditions derived by the above approximation are not suit­ able to accommodate the non-ideality behavior of water vapor or its competitive effect on the adsorption mechanism of benzene onto MOFs. In this context, the adsorption isotherms of benzene onto MOFs were estimated in this work based on the dynamic steady-state equilibrium (e. g., at $100\%$ BT saturation point) at each inlet benzene concentrations $(10{-}200\ \mathrm{ppm})$ across varying humidity conditions $_{(0-100\%)}$ RH). A similar procedure was also applied to analyze the dynamic adsorption isotherm of gaseous toluene in a fixed bed column of activated carbon [35,36]. Further, three kinetic models, pseudo-first-order (PFO), pseudo-second-order (PSO), and intra-particle diffusion (IPD), were also used to investigate the competitive effect of water molecules on the benzene adsorption rate and the rate-controlling step for mass transfer (via surface or intra-particle diffusion mechanisms) during the adsorp­ tion process. Afterward, the BT profiles across varying humidity levels were simulated using the Bohart-Adams (B-A), Thomas, and YoonNelson (Y–N) models to assess the mass transfer behavior of benzene onto the packed MOF adsorbents. The details of the isotherm and kinetic model equations, along with the results of their fittings, are provided in the SI. Note that OriginPro (2016) software was used to solve the nonlinear equations for the isotherm and kinetic models in this work. The suitability of those models for simulating the experimental adsorption data was also evaluated statistically using calculated corre­ lation coefficients $(\mathsf{R}^{2})$ and reduced chi-square test $(\chi^{2})$ analyses, as described in the SI. Moreover, the temperature effect on the nature of the benzene adsorption was also evaluated thermodynamically as detailed in Section 4 of SI.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Adsorption breakthrough profiles under dry conditions $(R H=O$
To learn more about the adsorption behavior of benzene on the two MOFs, their BT profiles were evaluated in various RH conditions. To begin with, the BT profiles under dry conditions $(\mathrm{RH}=0)\quad$ ) are evaluated for both MOF-199 and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ across various gaseous benzene concentrations $(10{-}200\ \mathrm{ppm})$ in Fig. 1 (a – b). Both MOFs showed similar BT profiles, with MOF-199 outperforming $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in terms of the BTV required to achieve a $100\%$ BT level at all tested benzene concentrations (Fig. 1 a – b). The saturation points (e.g., maximum ca­ pacity at $100\%$ BT) of the sorbent systems packed with MOF-199/UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were achieved after pulling approximately 20/5.4, 14.5/6.2, $10.2/3.0\$ , and $7.4/4.5\mathrm{L}$ of G-WS prepared at 10, 50, 100, and $200\mathrm{ppm}$ , respectively (Fig. S2a and b). At the $100\%$ BT stage, their $100\%$ BTV values were estimated to be ${>}4000/1075$ , 2904/1249, 2034/594, and 1483/891 L atm ${\bf g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Table 1). Compared with $100\%$ BTV, the $10\%$ BTV metric is a more meaningful criterion to assess the per­ formance of adsorbent packed bed from a practical viewpoint because it can be sensitively associated with the air quality the guideline [3]. In this regard, $10\%\mathrm{BTV}>100\mathrm{-L}$ atm ${\bf g}^{-1}$ has been suggested as a possible guideline for real-world adsorbent applications [37]. However, it should be noted that the dynamic adsorption of low-concentration benzene onto MOF-199 (at $\mathsf{10-p p m})$ and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (at 10 and $100\mathrm{-\ppm)}$ showed a moderate reduction in adsorption performance (e.g., adsorp­ tion increases then decreases) at the initial adsorption stage (in the re­ gion of $5\mathrm{-}20\%$ BT, Fig. 1). This adsorption drop could be attributed to the retrograde phenomenon, which is often related to the change of adsorption (adsorbent-adsorbate interaction) enthalpy during the adsorption process [38]. At this point, the retrograde phenomenon is expected to occur with the increase in the temperature (e.g., exothermic reaction) in association with the mass transfer at the sorption bed. More specifically, the interaction of adsorbent and adsorbate takes place rapidly on the fresh mass transfer zone or mass transfer resistance builds in a fixed bed adsorber [39–41]. Note that the extension of temporal resolution (e.g., from $0.1\ \mathrm{min}$ to $3\ \mathrm{min}\mathrm{.}$ ) may effectively dispel the retrograde phenomenon in the initial BT region, especially when there are strong and accelerated adsorbent-adsorbate interactions if the initial inlet feeding concentrations of benzene are significantly high [39]. In this case, it is suggested that MOF-199 could have a strong affinity for adsorption interaction with benzene than UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ as it does not experience retrograde phenomenon even at high inlet benzene concen­ trations (at $50{-}200~\mathrm{ppm}_{.}$ ).
The performance comparison was made using the $10\%$ BTV results, as shown in Table 1. The MOF-199 performance is far superior to that of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (by a factor of 1.9 to 16-fold differences) at all tested G-WS concentrations of benzene and $\mathrm{RH}=0$ . The trend in the $10\%$ BTV data is similarly seen in the other metrics. In case of PC (mol $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}\ \mathbf{Pa}^{-1})$ determined at $10\%$ BT, the MOF-199/UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ ratio values were 1.9–16. Further, the maximum capacity values (Q) of MOF-199 and UiO
