$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ at $200\mathrm{ppm}$ benzene were 191 and $58.8\mathrm{mg.}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. It should further be noted that only MOF-199 can be used practically for the adsorption of low-level benzene $(\leq50\mathrm{ppm})$ , with $10\%$ BTV values of 616–298 L atm ${\bf g}^{-1}$ (at $\mathrm{RH}=0\mathrm{\dot{_{\cdot}}}$ ), whereas the $10\%$ BTV values for UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were $122{-}35\mathrm{~L~}$ atm ${\bf g}^{-1}$ (Table 1). At higher inlet benzene con­ centrations $(>50$ to $200\mathrm{ppm},$ , both MOF-199 and UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ failed to achieve the reference $10\%$ BTV value required for practical applications (their $10\%$ BTV values ranged from 5.2 to $90\mathrm{~L~}$ atm ${\tt g}^{-1}$ , Table 1). Possible reasons for the decreased $10\%$ BTV for benzene uptake at higher concentrations are increased repulsive forces between the ben­ zene molecules adsorbed on the MOF surfaces and reduced availability of adsorption surface sites [3].
The superior adsorption performance of MOF-199 has three main causes: (i) MOF-199 has a higher BET surface area $(1212.2\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ than $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(963~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ; (ii) MOF-199 has high structural flexibility compared with the hard framework of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH_{2}};$ and (iii) MOF-199 has square, 4-coordinated, open, copper metal (Lewis acid) sites, whereas $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ has fully saturated zirconium sites [42,43]. These unique features of MOF-199 produce increased adsorption of benzene molecules by accelerating their surface and pore-diffusion through multi-cage channels, followed by chemical interaction with the open copper metal (Lewis acid) sites. Indeed, the Lewis acid in MOF-199 interacted preferentially with the $\pi$ -electron cloud of benzene (Lewis base) [44]. The possible interaction between benzene and the transition metal surface (Cu metal sites) was also supported by a density functional theory (DFT) analysis [45,46]. The DFT results indicate that the (110) and (111) facet surfaces of the Cu metal sites have high reactivity for interacting and binding with aromatic benzene molecules via physisorption-like and chemisorption-like processes (e.g., dispersive in nature with minimal covalent character).
