## 3.2. Effect of relative humidity and benzene concentrations on MOF adsorption performance
Fig. 2 (a – b) shows the competing relationships between benzene concentrations $(10{-}200\ \mathrm{ppm})$ and RH levels $(0-100\%)$ in relation to the adsorption performance of MOF-199 and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The results are compared in terms of adsorption capacity $\left(Q\colon\operatorname{mg}.g^{-1}\right)$ ) and PC $\mathrm{(mol~kg^{-1}}$ $\mathsf{P a}^{-1}\j$ values at $100\%$ BT. The maximum adsorption capacity (Q: mg. $g^{-1})$ of both MOFs increased significantly with the benzene concentra­ tion from 10 to $200\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , regardless of the RH $(0-100\%)$ . However, all performance metrics (BTV, Q, and PC) of both MOFs declined promi­ nently as the RH increased from zero to $50\%$ . However, it should be noted that the trend of declining adsorption performance was less apparent for both MOFs as RH increased further from 50 to $100\%$ (e.g., relative to lower $\mathrm{RHs}<50\%$ ), especially at benzene concentrations $\leq50$ ppm (Fig. 2 and Table 1). Compared with the results from ${\mathrm{RH}}= $ 0 (Fig. 2), the PC values $(\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\bar{\mathrm{\bfP}}\mathrm{a}^{-1})$ of both MOFs showed relatively steady-state curvatures under all humid conditions (i.e., $20\mathrm{-}100\%$ RH), regardless of benzene concentration $(10{-}200~\mathrm{ppm})$ . On the other hand, the adsorption performance of both MOFs, when expressed in terms of Q and BTV, depended on both the inlet benzene concentration and RH. As shown in Table 1, at $\mathrm{RH}\geq50\%$ , water vapor had a dramatic effect on benzene uptake, causing a larger decrease in the Q value $(85-99.9\%)$ ) of MOF-199 than in that of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(71-95\%)$ relative to the perfor­ mance data obtained at $\mathrm{RH}=0$ . Because physical diffusion and chem­ isorption on the surfaces of metal sites are the main adsorption sites for benzene capture on both MOFs, the reduced performances of the two MOFs can be explained by their relative selectivity for benzene as the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ levels increase.
Specifically, at RH $20\%$ $\mathrm{\Lambda}\approx6300\ p p m\ H_{2}0$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , the maximum capacity values (Q at $100\%$ BT) of the two MOFs were compared at 10 and $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ . The Q values of MOF-199 at those benzene concentration levels decreased dramatically, by $\sim86.8\%$ and $75\%$ , respectively, rela­ tive to that at $\mathrm{RH}=0$ . For $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , the reductions were $72.6\%$ and $49.1\%$ , respectively. These data indicate that $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ had better performance stability than MOF-199 when both RH and the benzene level are low. As the RH level increased from 20 to $100\%$ (i.e., from 6300 to 32,000 ppm $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , the adsorption behaviors of both MOFs were reduced further in terms of $10\%$ BTV $(<40\mathrm{~L~}$ atm ${\bf g}^{-1}$ ) and maximum Q $(<26\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ values (Table 1). Thus, these two MOFs are not useful for benzene adsorption when RH is $250\%$ and the benzene concentration is $\leq50$ ppm. On the other hand, at benzene concentrations of $100\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , an increase in RH from zero to $20\%$ caused relatively low reductions in the $10\%$ BTV capacity of MOF-199 and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (e.g., $22\%$ and $41\%$ , respectively) compared to high RHs (e.g., $\geq80\%$ RH). This contrasting trend indicates that an increase in benzene concentra­ tion can reduce the negative effects of water vapor on MOF performance when $\mathrm{RH}\leq20\%$ . At the much higher inlet G-WS benzene concentration of $200\mathrm{ppm}$ , the effectiveness of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for benzene adsorption (in terms of $10\%$ BTV) increased by two-fold at $20\%$ RH relative to dry conditions.
These findings demonstrate the possible existence of competition between water and benzene molecules for the sorption sites of MOFs [21,23,43]. However, the contribution of this competitive adsorption appears to depend on the concentration levels of both $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and benzene molecules, along with the properties of the MOF frameworks. In fact, it was previously reported that water uptake by MOFs depends mainly on the surface chemistry (hydrophobicity/hydrophilicity), porous struc­ tures and pore size, and reactivity of the active sites in MOF adsorbents [47]. Accordingly, it can be inferred that the competition for sorption sites when the inlet benzene is high $(>100\ \mathrm{ppm})$ and the RH is low $\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\left(<\aftergroup\egroup\right)$ $20\%$ ) could reduce the physical and chemical interactions (e.g., diffu­ sion, H-bonding, or coordination mechanisms) between water molecules and each MOF as their surface hydrophobicity increases. In those con­ ditions, the partial occupation of pores with water molecules can decrease the number of adsorption sites or pore-volume freely available for benzene adsorption (e.g., slightly reduce benzene adsorption ca­ pacity). In humid conditions of ${>}50\%$ RH (i.e., in the presence of $>15,890\mathrm{\ppm\H_{2}O)}$ , the unsaturated Cu-metal sites and hydrophilic oxygen groups in the BTC linker of MOF-199 have a stronger affinity for water molecules than for benzene molecules [23]. Thus, the uptake of water molecules by MOF-199 might destroy its inner porous structure (distort the framework structure) through axial coordinative in­ teractions between $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and Cu metal sites that subsequently reduce the external contact surface sites (i.e., BET surface area) [48]. In contrast, UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ has eight fully saturated zirconium $\left(\mathrm{Zr}\right)$ metal sites, which confirms its low affinity for interaction with benzene or water mole­ cules. Thus, pore-filling could be the mechanism for benzene/water adsorption onto UiO-66- $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ . In that case, the existence of hydrophilic functional amine groups in the porous $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ structure could promote hydrogen bonding with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in all humid conditions $\left(\mathrm{RH}\geq\right.$ $20\%$ ) [47]. Such bonding interactions should increase with the water vapor pressure , reducing the pore-filling pressure of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ available for benzene uptake through irreversible capillary condensa­ tion of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ molecules in the porous framework. Meanwhile, it should be noted that the affinity of $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{-NH_{2}}}$ groups in UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ can be altered to interact more favorably with benzene molecules than $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ only at an inlet benzene concentration $>100$ ppm and $\mathrm{RH}<50\%$ . This observation is, in fact, in line with reported decreases in the negative effects of RH on the performance of MOFs [49]. Under this condition, the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups (Lewis basic sites) and the aromatic ring of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC linker in UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ appear to contribute greatly to the improvement of adsorption selectivity for benzene as $\pi$ -rich guest molecules (relative to $\mathrm{H_{2}O}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ via strong $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and $\mathrm{C-H}\cdots\pi$ hydrogen bonding interactions [50–52]. Therefore, at $\mathrm{RH}>20\%$ , interactions between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules and both MOFs explain the rapid decline in the MOFs adsorption capacities for benzene molecules at both low and high inlet concentra­ tions, either through pore-filling physisorption phenomena or chem­ isorptive interactions with the open Cu-metal sites in MOF-199 or the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups in UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ .
## 3.3. Adsorption Isotherm Analyses
The adsorption behaviors of benzene onto MOF-99 and UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ were investigated as a function of RH levels $(0-100\%)$ using three nonlinearized isotherm models $(\mathrm{L},\mathrm{F},$ and DR). Table 2 illustrates the calculated constants and fitting data $(\mathsf{R}^{2}$ and $\chi^{2})$ for the L, F, and DR isotherm models. Based on the $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ criterion, the three isotherm models exhibited good fitting with the experimental adsorption data for ben­ zene onto MOF-199 or $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ at the tested RH levels $(0.90<\mathrm{R}^{2}<$ 1). Only at ${\mathrm{RH}}=0$ , the DR model showed a low fitting tendency with the adsorption isotherm data of benzene on both MOFs (MOF-199 $(\mathbf{R}^{2}=\mathbf{\Phi}$ 0.68) and UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.855)$ , Table 2). Due to the high $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ values (Table 2), the quantitative error function $(\chi^{2})$ term was used to deter­ mine the best fitting isotherms based on the relative errors between the experimental and predicted equilibrium adsorption capacities data.
Overall, at $\mathrm{RH}=0$ (Fig. 3), the L and F models exhibited a better fit for the benzene adsorption isotherm on UiO-66-NH2 $(\chi^{2}=5.2)$ and MOF-199 $(\chi^{2}$ of 36.7), respectively. These observations demonstrate the favorability of each model for benzene at $\mathrm{RH}\:=\:0$ : a multi-layer adsorption mechanism for MOF-199 (F-model fit) and monolayer adsorption on UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (L-model fit) (Fig. 3). Notably, the optimal fitting pattern for MOF-199 changed as a function of RH: the DR model $(\chi^{2}=30$ and 0.11) was optimal at $\mathrm{RH}=20\%$ and $\mathrm{RH}=100\%$ ; the Lmodel $(\chi^{2}=0.53)$ was best at $\mathrm{RH}=50\%$ ; and the F-model $(\chi^{2}=1.2)$ was best at $\mathrm{RH}=80\%$ . Likewise, benzene adsorption onto UiO-66- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ exhibited a disordered fitness with the three isotherm models as a function of RH: the F-model $(\chi^{2}=4.6$ and 0.31) was the best fit at ${\mathrm{RH}}= $ $20\%$ and $\mathrm{RH}=100\%$ ; and the DR model $(\chi^{2}=0.56\ –0.35)$ fitted best at $\mathrm{RH}=50{-}80\%$ . The disordered adsorption isotherm fitting for benzene onto the MOF-199/UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ adsorbents could result from alterations in the adsorption mechanisms as a function of RH due to the competitive inhibition caused by binary $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ /benzene components during adsorp tion at various RH/benzene concentration levels.
The DR model was used to estimate the mean adsorption energy (E, $\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}.$ ) for benzene onto both MOF adsorbents and explain the mechanism of benzene adsorption in relation to RH, as summarized in Table 2. The values of E $\mathrm{(kJ\mol^{-1}}.$ ) for benzene onto MOF-199 were higher than those onto UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ , supporting the enhanced reactivity of Cu-metal sites for benzene uptake compared with $z\mathrm{r}$ sites. Nonethe­ less, the computed E values $(\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1})$ for MOF-199 decreased notice­ ably from 1.1 to $0.280\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ as the RH rose from zero to $20\%$ , respectively. A similar pattern was observed in UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . These data indicated that water molecules seem to compete with benzene to occupy the active/open metal sites (e.g., Cu) or functional moieties (e.g., $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ at low and high humidity conditions. It is thus suggested that the physical pore-filling phenomenon is the dominant mechanism for ben­ zene adsorption onto both porous MOF adsorbents in all humid condi­ tions. This suggestion was vividly supported by the heterogeneity factor (n) of the F-model, which decreased from 2.1 to 0.9 for MOF-199 and from 2.2 to 0.75 for UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ as the RH increased from 0 to $100\%$ , respectively. That observation indicates the reduction in heterogeneous sorption sites on both MOF surfaces in the presence of water vapor. Especially if $n<1$ (at $\mathrm{RH}>80\%$ , benzene molecules should be loosely bonded to both MOFs due to the prominence of adsorbed water mole­ cules in altering the MOF surface chemistry (i.e., increasing surface hydrophilicity). This supposition is supported by the high affinity of MOF-199 and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for water adsorption, with maximum water capacities of 594 and $450~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ , respectively, at $\mathrm{RH}=90\%$ [53].
In microporous MOF-199, the unsaturated copper sites act as Lewis acid metal centers (partially positive charges on $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ metal sites) to increase the electrostatic attraction (or coordination bonding) to water vapor with a partial δ-negative charge near the O-atom. Such electro­ static/coordination bonding could distort the MOF-199 framework as a result of ligand replacement or hydrolysis caused by coordination be­ tween the $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and Cu–Cu secondary building blocks (SBU) in MOF-199 [23]. This type of distortion in the SBU can also help explain the disordered isotherm modeling results. However, such distortion might not easily break the integrity of Cu-carboxylate bonds unless MOF-199 is put under harsh conditions (e.g., $\mathrm{RH}\geq90\%$ at temperatures above $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ [54]. This was also confirmed by naked-eye observations of color changes in MOF-199 from navy blue to light cyan during adsorption in humid conditions, which returned to navy blue after thermal condi­ tioning. This can also be verified by examining the structural integrity of MOF-199 before and after benzene exposure at RH 0, 50, and $100\%$ , as explained in Section 3.5.
Based on the experimental and isotherm data obtained in this work, it is suggested that the outer hydrophilic ${\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ group on UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ should preferably interact with water over benzene at low levels of both RH $(\leq20\%)$ and benzene $\mathrm{\Sigma(\leq50\ppm)}$ . On the other hand, pore-blocking with water molecules appears to be the main cause of the reduction in benzene adsorption onto $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ at high RH $(>50\%)$ , regardless of the tested concentration levels of benzene $(10{-}200~\mathrm{ppm})$ . At that harsh and humid condition, the sorption sites $\boldsymbol{(\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ or Cu sites or pore diffu­ sion) on both MOF adsorbents have higher affinities for water vapor than for benzene molecules.
