adsorption kinetic rates of benzene and the saturation of the adsorbent systems packed with MOF-199 and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ are shown in Figs. 4 and 5, respectively. Notably, the equilibrium time to achieve $100\%$ BT ca­ pacity for benzene on both MOF adsorbents decreased noticeably with an increase in either the inlet benzene concentration or RH. At ${\mathrm{RH}}=0$ , $100\%$ BT capacity for benzene onto UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was achieved after shorter contact times $\ensuremath{\mathrm{(1-10~min}}$ , Fig. 5) than onto MOF-199 $\mathrm{10{-}60\ m i n}$ , Fig. 4) at all benzene concentrations $(10{-}200\mathrm{ppm})$ . As the RH increased from zero to $100\%$ , the time to achieve $100\%$ BT capacities for 10–200 ppm benzene was reduced to $<0.3$ and $<0.5$ min for MOF-199 and UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , respectively. These observations confirm the highly negative effects that water molecules have on benzene adsorption by both MOFs, especially MOF-199.
## 3.4.1. Surface/diffusion kinetic modeling
The experimental kinetic patterns of benzene adsorption were investigated using PFO, PSO, and IPD kinetic models to predict the ratecontrolling steps for both MOF adsorbents. The results of these compu­ tations are derived as a function of inlet benzene concentration vs. hu­ midity and summarized in Table 3.
At $\mathrm{RH}=0\%$ , the tendency of the kinetic fit varied sensitively with changes in the inlet benzene concentration. The adsorption of benzene on both MOF-199 and UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ favorably followed the PFO model $(\mathsf{R}^{2}\colon0.995–0.999$ and $\chi^{2}$ $\cdot0.0912\ –2.14\}$ at the benzene concentrations of $10{-}50\ \mathrm{ppm}$ (Table 3). In contrast, at the highest benzene concentrations $(100{-}200~\mathrm{ppm})$ ), the optimal kinetic fitting of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ matched the PSO model $\left(\mathrm{R}^{2}\colon0.985–0.991\right.$ and $1.77\%<\chi^{2}<5.33)$ , not the PFO model. On the other hand, under humid conditions $(\mathrm{RH}=20\mathrm{-}100\%)$ , the kinetic fit quality changed as a function of RH, regardless of the tested benzene concentration $(10{-}200\ \mathrm{ppm})$ . In this case, the PFO model always exhibited a better fit for U $\mathrm{iO-66-NH_{2}R^{2}}$ of 0.951–0.997 and $\chi^{2}$ of 0.0346–1.19), and the PSO-model fitted better for MOF-199 $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ of 0.987–0.998 and $\chi^{2}$ of 0.01–2.63) as the RH rose to $20\%$ . At RH $50\%$ , the PFO model yielded the best fit to the experimental adsorption data at
