$200\mathrm{ppm}$ benzene $(\mathrm{R}^{2}{:0.991-0.999}$ and $\chi^{2}$ $^{2}\colon0.082–0.175)$ for both MOFs, but that was distorted as the RH rose to ${\geq}80\%$ (low fitness with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{R}}^{2}<$ 0.869 and $\chi^{2}$ up to 45.5) at all G-WS concentrations (Table 3). The re­ sults of this comparison suggest the dominant role played by the phys ical adsorption of benzene on both MOFs in the dry gaseous stream. However, at $20\%$ RH, adsorption of both MOFs takes a more complex pattern due to the contribution of active sites (the open Cu-metal sites or $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups) in the adsorption of both benzene and water molecules. More specifically, the failure of the kinetic models used to predict ben­ zene adsorption by both MOFs at $\mathrm{RH}>50\%$ can be attributed to the alteration of their adsorption affinity and selectivity from benzene (poor capacity) to water, along with abnormal BT behavior at the initial BT regions (i.e., the roll-up effect, see Section 3.4.2 and Figs. S3 and S4).
The IPD kinetic model was used to predict the rate-controlling steps for benzene adsorption on both MOFs under all tested conditions. As seen in Figs. 6 and 7, almost all of the linear IPD curvatures (Q $\mathsf{\Omega}(\mathrm{mg}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ vs. t $(\mathrm{min})^{0.5})$ for adsorbed benzene onto both MOFs displayed three linear kinetic stages without passing through the origin point at any tested condition. This indicates the complex adsorption process of benzene onto MOFs, which can include surface/pore-diffusion and chemisorptive interactions [55]. Based on the IPD kinetic data in Table S2, the values of $\mathrm{K_{id}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{i}}$ (pore and surface diffusivity constants, respectively) increased with the inlet concentration of benzene at ${\mathrm{RH}}={}$ 0. In this case, the increase in the inlet partial pressure of benzene could promote benzene diffusion from the boundary surface layer into the pores of both MOFs, leading to improved adsorption performance under dry conditions. An increase in RH from 0 to $20\%$ led to a decrease in the values of the IPD diffusion constants $\mathrm{\DeltaK_{id}}$ and Ci) (Table S2). The observed results could reflect competitive diffusion between benzene and water molecules; partial pore-filling with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules could reduce the volume of pores freely available for benzene uptake. At $\mathrm{RH}>$ $80\%$ , the $\mathrm{K_{id}}$ constant turned from positive to negative at the 2nd and 3rd IPD stages on both MOFs. Thus, the surface/film diffusion of ben­ zene (e.g., physicochemical interactions with active surface sites) on both MOFs should be the sole rate-limiting adsorption step, and it results from complete pore-filling with water vapor molecules (i.e., a negligible pore-diffusion mechanism for benzene at $\mathrm{RH}>80\%$ .
