The relationship between the operational parameters (especially the water vapor and benzene concentrations) and the dynamic BT adsorp­ tion behaviors of benzene onto packed-bed MOF adsorbents was also investigated using three breakthrough models (B-A, Thomas, and Y-N: the hypotheses and equations used for these models are described in the SI). The BT fitting results and estimated packed bed parameters derived from these three models are listed in Table S3. As summarized in Table S3, all these BT models exhibit a high fitting trend with the experimental adsorption BT data for MOF-199 $(0.97<\mathrm{R}^{2}<1\$ and 2.1E$0\Bar{4}<\chi^{2}<4.6\mathrm{E}\cdot03)$ and UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH_{2}}}$ 1 $0.96<\mathrm{R}^{2}<1$ and 3. $\scriptstyle\sum-04<\chi^{2}<$ 5.4E-03) at all tested conditions, with the exception of $10\mathrm{ppm}$ benzene on MOF-199 under RH $100\%$ (poor fitting, with $\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.80\mathrm{\Omega}$ . That poor fit
B. Liu et al.
can be attributed to strong roll-up behavior in the initial stage of adsorption, with a maximum BT profile $(\mathrm{BT}\approx119\%)$ ) at a low benzene concentration under $\mathrm{RH}\geq80\%$ (Fig. S4). The roll-up effect has been observed in multi-component adsorption systems as weakly adsorbed molecules (e.g., benzene) are displaced by strongly interacting water molecules [43]. Although the occurrence of the roll-up phenomenon cannot be ruled out at higher concentrations of benzene, it is unlikely to affect the fitness of the data between the experimental results and BT kinetic models. Based on Thomas model data, some divergent results in the estimated maximum capacities vs. experimental capacities (QTh/ Qe: Table S3) revealed that the adsorption of benzene onto both MOFs may not follow the assumption well made by Thomas model, especially at some G-WS concentrations under high RHs (e.g., 10 ppm under $100\%$ RH, QTh/Qe: 22.9). Accordingly, the axial dispersion and random mo­ tion of benzene vapor flowing through the active mass transfer zones (MTZs) on both fixed-bed MOFs might have significantly affected the adsorption kinetic rates of benzene under all tested conditions. Such random movement could be caused by any of several factors: (i) the combined influence of G-WS concentration and RH on the molecular diffusivity of benzene (e.g., competitive adsorption process) through the active MTZ layers in the fixed-bed MOFs, (ii) the negative effects of humidity on bed porosity and the particle diameter of MOFs, and (iii) the presence of heterogeneous active adsorbent sites with a high affinity for adsorbate molecules (multilayer adsorption mechanisms).
From the BT kinetic data (Table S3), it is clear that the kinetic rates $(\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{BA}}$ or $\mathrm{K_{Th}}\mathrm{:}\mathbf{m}^{3}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{m}\mathrm{in}^{-1}\mathrm{:}$ for benzene uptake by MOF-199 and UiO-66- $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ increased with RH at all G-WS concentrations. In contrast, a reverse relationship was shown between the values of the $\mathrm{K_{BA}}$ or $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{Th}}$ kinetic rates and inlet G-WS concentrations, regardless of RH levels. Those observations confirm the reduced capacity of MOF-199 and UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ for benzene uptake as a result of water adsorption on the MTZ layers and the high competition between the two adsorbate molecules on the adsorbent surfaces (i.e., decreases in diffusion rate). These possibilities are supported further by a dramatic decrease in the estimated $50\%$ BT service lifetimes of the MOF beds (using the Y–N model): $\boldsymbol{\tau}$ -values of $4.5{-}22~\mathrm{min}$ for MOF-199 and $0.82\mathrm{-}5.8\ \mathrm{min}$ for $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ at ${\mathrm{RH}}= $ 0 were lowered to $<1.0$ min when $\mathrm{RH}\geq50\%$ at all tested G-WS con­ centrations. Based on these observations, MOF-199 shows moderately enhanced potential under dry conditions for benzene uptake at a high inlet vapor pressure (e.g., from industrial/natural gaseous streams), with more extended service lifetime (Table S3). However, the use of this MOF adsorbent under humid conditions (especially against aromatic VOCs) is not recommendable due to the degradation or collapse of its framework in the presence of water vapor (see Section 3.6.2).
