## 3.5. Thermodynamic consideration on adsorption of benzene
The adsorption behavior of benzene by both MOFs was also studied across varying temperature conditions both under dry and humid con­ ditions to analyze the the effects of temperatures on thermodynamic basis. The relationship between maximum capacities vs. the loaded volume is plotted in Figs. S5to explore the effects of temperatures (25, 40, and $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ on the adsorption of gaseous benzene (at $50\mathrm{ppm})$ by both MOFs under dry (zero RH) and humid $100\%$ RH) conditions. Under the dry condtion (Figs. S5 (a-b)), the adsorption capacities of both MOFs decreased dramatically (e.g., by more than $36~\%$ ) with the increase of temperature from 25 to $60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ supporting an exothermic adsorption process for benzene. Such observation could be attributed to the enhanced activity of benzene molecules with the increase of tempera­ tures so as to lower the adsorption performances of both MOFs.
It was interesting to find that the benzene adsorption patterns onto both MOFs were reversed at varying temperatures in the presence of moisture (Figs. S5(c) and (d)) when the results were compared to dry condition (Figs. S5(a) and (b)). At $100\%$ RH, the maximum adsorption capacities increased 5.3 and 6.6-fold with the enhancement of temper­ atures from 25 to $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for MOF-199 and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , respectively. Under dry condition, the patterns of $\Delta G$ values contrasted with increases in temperature such as noticeable rise in MOF-199 and almost no effect on UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}$ (Table S4 and Fig. S6). The above results clearly indicate
B. Liu et al.
that the adsorption behavior is more favorable at lower temperatures for MOF-199, while temperature effect is less eminent for $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . Meanwhile, the negative $\Delta\mathrm{H}$ values of both MOF-199 $(-50.5\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ ) and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(-20.5\mathrm{~\textmu~{~}~m o l^{-1})}$ consistently suggested the exothermic nature of the adsorption process [56]. Further, the $\Delta s$ values were found as negatively and positively for MOF-199 $(-0.084~\mathrm{kJ}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.$ ) and UiO- $\cdot66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH_{2}}(0.007\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{K^{-1}m o l^{-1}})$ , respectively. As such, the decreased trend of randomness is apparent for MOF-199 at the adsorbent-gas interface during the adsorption (in contrast to the increased disorder in $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\rangle$ [57]. At $100\%$ RH condition, ΔG values were seen to descrease continuously for both MOFs with increasing temperatures. These observations suggest that the adsorption processes should proceed spontaneously. Further, it was found that the positive values were seen consistenly in both ΔH (MOF199: 49.9 and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ : $59.7~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}\cdot$ and ΔS (MOF199: 0.22 and UiO- $66\small{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ : $0.25\mathrm{\kJ\K^{-1}\ m o l^{-1})}$ at $100\%$ RH. Consequently, these patterns are indicative of the endothermic nature of benzene adsorption by these MOFs with the increasing disorder at the solid-gas interface.
## 3.6. Performance comparison and adsorption mechanism
## 3.6.1. Performance comparison with previous studies
The adsorption performance of MOF-199 and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ toward benzene reported in this work was compared with that of other pub­ lished sorbents, as listed in Table S5. To date, little attention has been paid to the combinatorial effects of target VOC pollutants (e.g., benzene) and competing components (e.g., water vapor) on the adsorption per­ formance of each adsorbent at different BT regions. Note that in most studies, the adsorption performance of the tested adsorbents was generally confined to $100\%$ BT adsorption capacity. As such, researchers claimed superior benzene uptake by several MOF adsorbents based on their maximum capacities, such as MIL(Cr)-Z1 $(-546\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1})}$ [58], MIL-101 $(293\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ [21], MOF-199 $\cdot780\mathrm{\mg\g{-}}1\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ [23], and CIBE-1.5 $(1392~\mathrm{mg~g{-}1)}$ [59], but those studies were carried out at high partial pressure conditions for benzene (e.g., 870 to $6\ensuremath{\mathrm{\kPa}}_{\mathcal{A}}$ ). The problems associated with such biased conditions have been discussed in detail elsewhere [37]. To provide a more meaningful evaluation of the per­ formance of different materials, our experimental data are based on more objective metrics (e.g., PC, $10\%$ BTV, and capacity ratio $(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{humid}}/$ $\ensuremath{Q_{\mathrm{dry}}}).$ ) as a function of partial pressure ratios of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ : benzene vapors $(\mathrm{P_{H2O}/P_{B n z}})$ .
Based on the $\mathrm{Q_{humid}/Q_{d r y}}$ metric, most MOFs and carbon-based ad­ sorbents preserved their reported maximum capacities with a rise in RH from 5 to $90\%$ at high inlet benzene concentrations (500–5000 ppm; Table S5) due to the decrease in $\mathrm{P_{H2O}/P_{B n z}}$ ratios (0.8–48). Accordingly, the negative effect of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor on the adsorption performance (e.g., $10\%$ BTV and maximum adsorption capacity) of MOFs or AC toward a high benzene concentration at low $\mathrm{P_{H2O}/P_{B n z}}$ ratios can be diminished, as illustrated in Sections 3.2–3.4. Most of the so-called excellent sorbents in the literature (e.g., MSN-100 and CAS-2 based on their high maximum adsorption capacities) were not satisfactory for removing airborne benzene even at the dry condition when their performances were assessed in terms of their $10\%$ BTV (7.5–68 L atm ${\bf g}^{-1}.$ ) and PC (2.1E$03{-}0.057\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{Pa}^{-1})$ values (Table S5). On a comparative note, in this study, UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ showed that water had a lower effect on benzene adsorption in terms of $\mathrm{Q_{humid}/Q_{d r y}(5.4\mathrm{-}73.1\%)}$ than it did on MOF-199 $(0.02\mathrm{-}56.9\%)$ under all tested conditions $(\mathrm{P_{H2O}/P_{B n z}}$ of 32–634). How­ ever, it should be noted that the $\mathrm{Q_{humid}/Q_{d r y}}$ values and relative ratios of $\mathrm{P_{H2O}/P_{B n z}}$ have no direct relationship, as listed in Table S5. Thus, the adsorption performance of MOFs for benzene in the presence of water vapor could be affected by multiple factors in addition to the competi­ tive inhibition mechanism between water and benzene molecules. Those factors could include adsorbent stability, surface chemistry, and the affinity/selectivity of adsorption sites for adsorbate molecules $\mathrm{(H_{2}O}$ and benzene). This observation is supported by the high capacity of the AC adsorbent series to maintain noticeably enhanced adsorption performance $\mathrm{(Q_{humid}/Q_{d r y}}$ of $13\mathrm{-}85\%$ ) at a low benzene concentration $(5~\mathrm{ppm})$ and high RH (i.e., $\mathrm{P_{H2O}/P_{B n z}}$ of 3170–4438, Table S5). Such enhanced potential can be accounted for by the high chemical stability of the AC adsorbent series, even under highly humid conditions, along with their high surface hydrophobicity that favorably attracts aromatic benzene while repelling polar water molecules. These observations thus support the promising applicability of those AC sorbents for real-world applications. On this note, future research should be directed toward the development of MOF sorbents with high chemical stability and surface chemistry modified for the selective adsorption of target VOCs such as benzene over water vapor to effectively improve their air puri­ fication capability.
## 3.6.2. Reusability, structural stability, and adsorption mechanism
To evaluate the suitability of the two MOFs as adsorbents for benzene in a practical sense, the reusability was also studied over three adsorption-desorption cycles against $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ benzene at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ All of these expepriments were conducted under both dry $\mathrm{(RH=0\%}$ ) and wet conditions ( $50\%$ and $100\%$ RH). During the reusability study, the spent MOF sorbents were regenerated under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ after each cycle. As seen in Fig. S6, both MOFs showed high adsorption stability under dry conditions, with negligible changes in the maximum adsorption capacity even after two reuse cycles (i.e., MOF-199 (cycle I: $99.6~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and cycle II: $102.2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (cycle I: 35.6 $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and cycle II: $35.1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ). However, both MOFs demonstrated a considerable drop in the adsorption capacity in the third cycle by about $21.3\substack{-44.1\%}$ (i.e., MOF-199 $(78.3~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ 1 $(19.9~\mathrm{mg}\$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1})}\end{array}$ ). Such observation could be explained at least partly by irreversible behavior during the sorption cycles and/or by the collapse of framework textural properties structure during reuse cycle, as confirmed in previ­ ous studies [60,61]. As shown in Fig. S7, the two MOFs spent under 50 and $100\%$ RH continuously showed low maxiumum adsorption capac­ ities $(<7~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . Thus, MOFs with strong chemical and water-stable properties may have to be developed to overcome these limiations in future work. In addition, it should be noted that both MOF-199 and UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ showed high water adsorption capacities at $100\%$ RH such as 360 and $120\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}$ , respectively, while such capacities were almost negligible at low RHs $\textcircled{5}50\%$ RH) (Table S6). These findings of high water affinities can reflect the strong competitiveness between water and benzene molecules at high RH conditions.
The structural stability of the MOF adsorbents after they’d adsorbed benzene $(200\ \mathrm{ppm})$ was evaluated using Fourier transform-infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) character­ ization data obtained under both dry and humidified conditions $\mathrm{RH}=$ 0, 50, and $100\%$ , as described in Section 1.2 in the SI. In Fig. S8 (a), it is evident that the FTIR spectra of MOF-199 showed similar patterns be­ tween before and after exposure to $200~\mathrm{ppm}$ G-WS benzene at varying RH conditions (0, 50, and $100\%$ ), without noticeable shifts in the main characteristic peaks. Only the intensity of the C-O-Cu stretching vibra­ tion peaks at 728 and $760~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ decreased slightly at RH 50 and $100\%$ (relative to fresh MOF-199 before adsorption), possibly due to the coordinative interaction between water molecules and the Cu-metal sites. Furthermore, a small shift in the asymmetrical modes of the carboxylate group from 1639 (pristine MOF-199) to $1643~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (spent MOF-199 at RH 0, 50, and $100\%$ ) indicates the contribution of an O$\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ group in the adsorption process. In addition, the appearance of a new peak between 1600 and $1480~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C})$ verifies the successful adsorption of gaseous benzene onto MOF-199. In the case of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Fig. S8 (b)), most of the characteristic FTIR peaks were not altered after benzene adsorption at any RH condition, including N–H wagging of the secondary amides $(765~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ), C–N stretching vibrations (1258 and $1338\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ), $\mathtt{C}{=}0$ ( $1568~\mathrm{cm}^{-1};$ , N–H $(1699~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1})}$ , and the $\tt N-H$ single bond vibration $(3360~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ . Note that the adsorption of benzene is not clearly reflected on the spent UiO- ${\cdot66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ , possibly due to its low capacity for benzene or interference between the stretching vibration peaks of the aromatic ring (e.g., $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}$ at $1585{-}1600\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$
B. Liu et al.
in-plane bending at $1000{-}1250\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) and the absorption bands of UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (e.g., $\mathtt{C=}0$ , C–N, and N–H). Those FTIR data confirm that the adsorption mechanism for gaseous benzene on MOF-199 could proceed via the coordination of benzene molecules with the unsaturated Cu and $0.0{=}0$ sites in the MOF structure, in addition to the pore-diffusion process. On the other hand, the adsorption process for benzene on $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is controlled mainly by the surface/pore-diffusion mechanism.
In the powder XRD patterns (Fig. S9), the main diffraction peaks of MOF-199 are visible before and after benzene exposure at all humidified conditions, including a 2θ of $11.6^{\circ}$ (222), $13.4^{\circ}$ (400), $14.6^{\circ}$ (331), $17.4^{\circ}$ (333), $19.0^{\circ}$ (440), and $29.4^{\circ}$ (662). However, the intensity of these diffraction peaks decreased as the RH increased from zero to $100\%$ indicating the presence of minor structural changes caused by degra­ dation of the inner structure via coordination between the open Cumetal sites and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules during benzene adsorption under hu­ mified conditions [48]. Those structural changes in MOF-199 are likely the main reason for its remarkable reduction in performance toward benzene adsorption under all humid conditions. Therefore, spent MOF199 cannot be reused for further adsorption-desorption cycles of ben­ zene under humidified conditions. Unlike MOF-199, UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ pos­ sesses a robust framework structure with a fully saturated Zr-SBU [54]. The fully saturated $z\mathrm{r}$ -sites are useful to enhance the chemical stability of UiO- $66\small{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ against water molecules (i.e., no coordinative interaction between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $z\mathrm{r}$ sites). Therefore, the related powder XRD patterns from UiO- ${\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ before and after adsorption were not studied because $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was expected to maintain its structural integrity under all tested conditions. This expectation was supported in previous studies, in which the effectiveness of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was demonstrated in the adsorptive removal of various target VOCs in the presence of water without structural deformation [62,63]. However, in this work, it was confirmed that the capacity of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for benzene adsorption declined dramatically under low and high humidity conditions (i.e., unsatisfactory adsorption performance) due to the competitive inhibi­ tion of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ molecules on the diffusion of benzene.
## 4. Conclusion
To date, a good number of studies have been carried out to assess the capacity of MOF adsorbents in the adsorption of airborne benzene in the presence of water molecules in air. The reliability of those studies has often been questioned, as the adsorption capacity of the tested materials is estimated under the extreme conditions to yield unrealistically exaggerated adsorption capacities by maintaining significantly high feeding concentrations of the target compound. The biased results derived under such controlled conditions can mislead the general as­ pects of competition that may take place to occupy adsorption sites between benzene and water molecues under real-world conditions. To learn more about the diverse aspects of competition conditions in the adsorption of gaseous benzene, we conducted comprehensive studies in which the adsorption capability of a given target is evaluated between different MOFs (e.g., MOF-199 and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ under varying RH conditions.
In this research, MOF-199 and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ were synthesized as model adsorbents to explore their adsorption potential against gaseous benzene with its concentrations of 10 to $200~\mathrm{ppm}$ in the presence of moisture in termd of RH (at $0\%$ , $20\%$ , $50\%$ , $80\%$ , and $100\%$ ). Due to its unique flexibile properties, MOF-199 was a far better sorbent for removing low and high benzene concentrations than UiO- $66\small{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in dry conditions. However, the performance of both MOFs decreased dramatically under humidified conditions, especially at low benzene concentrations ${\leq}50~\mathrm{ppm}$ . Such decrease in adsorption capability is attributed to the combined effects of various factors: (i) the enhanced selectivity of MOFs for water over benzene via a competitive diffusion mechanism, (ii) the high reactivity of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ with the active sorption sites of the MOFs $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ and Cu sites), and (iii) the low water stability of MOF
199 under humid conditions. Therefore, the experimental adsorption data for both MOFs obtained under humid conditions showed poor fits with kinetic and isotherm models, reflecting the competing role of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ vapor for their available sorption sites.
Overall, the kinetic and isotherm modeling data obtained from a series of experiments indicate that the intraparticle diffusion plays an important role in the adsorption of benzene at dry and low RH $(<50\%)$ , whereas surface interaction is the rate-limiting adsorption process at RH $\geq80\%$ (i.e., pore-filling with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules). Further, the unsaturated Cu metal (in MOF-199) and $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{2}}$ groups (in $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) could contribute to the enhanced adsorption process of benzene at dry con­ ditions. Further, the Bohart-Adams, Thomas, and Yoon-Nelson models showed good agreement with the experimental adsorption data at all tested conditions, demonstrating that BT modeling can help predict the performance of MOF fixed-bed systems at different relative vapor pressures of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ : benzene $(\mathrm{P_{H2O}/P_{B n z}})$ . The thermodynamic analysis also elucidated the exothermic adsorption behavior of benzen onto both MOFs at dry conditions, while endothermic nature was evident under the humid conditions. Based on the performance comparison at various ratios of $\mathrm{P_{H20}/P_{B n z}},$ the performance of MOF adsorbents was found to be dependent on many variables, including the chemical stability of the MOF, surface chemistry (porosity, functional sites, and hydrophobicity/ hydrophilicity), and the $\mathrm{P_{H2O}/P_{B n z}}$ ratio in the influent gaseous stream. In this case, to improve the performance of MOF-based air purification, it is essential (i) to increase the surface hydrophobicity to upgrade the selectivity for the target adsorbate and (ii) to design robust MOFs with high chemical stability for the protection of their texture and fixed-bed porosity (diffusion process) against water vapor under the humid envi­ ronmental conditions.
